From c5615d9c7b78b085f88b81f7dd27816e86eec3bc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 05:38:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] find a name by its accented letters however the name stores
 them
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A letter with an accent has two spellings that look the same. `é` can
be one character, which is what a keyboard types, or an `e` followed
by its accent, which is how macOS stores it in the names made in
Finder and in most Mac apps. fff-search compares a search with the
names byte for byte, and the engine's own checks compared them
character for character, so the two spellings never matched. Typed
with its accent, `señor` did not list a `señor.txt` stored the other
way when any other name had the word. Alone, such a name was found
only through the typo fallback: among fff-search's far misses, without
a highlight, and not at all with Allow typos off, in a quoted word, or
when it was an app's. A name copied out of Finder into the search
field failed the other way round, against names that store each
accented letter as one character.

This gives fffinder-core a direct dependency, which is the owner's
call: icu_normalizer 2, without its default features and with
`compiled_data`. It is no new crate to the app. Cargo.lock has it at
2.4.0 already, built with exactly those features for the UI kit (gpui
needs it through url and idna), so Cargo.lock gains one line and the
app compiles nothing it didn't. What it links in addition is at most
the plain decomposition table and its supplement, about 100 KB as
source text; the size of a release binary was not measured.
unicode-normalization would have been the usual small crate, and a
new one.

What fff-search is asked: `spellings` gives the search as it was
typed, with each such letter as one character, and with each as the
letter and then its accent, as far as these differ. `Engine::search`
makes the search in each, in lowercase and as typed as it does for
capitals, and `keep_higher` merges what they find in a search
location. A search in ASCII has one spelling and is made as before. A
search for one letter is not made again: `search_root` looks through
the names for the letter itself, in either spelling. What a search
leaves out with `!` is in each of its searches in every spelling,
since a name left out of one search would come back with another.
`track_open` remembers a search under the text of each of them, so a
file opened from a search is looked up by the one that finds it.

What the engine compares: `as_compared` puts text in lowercase and in
one spelling (NFC). It takes the place of the lowercasing wherever a
search's words meet a name: the path a hit is checked in
(`RawHit::rel`), the words, the paths `changes_for` goes by, the app
names in `app_hits` and `case_fit`, the names `rerank`, `name_has` and
`name_holds` look at, and quoted phrases. A Korean name stored as the
parts of its syllables is found by the syllables for the same reason.
`may_be_spelled_apart` is gone. Names have the words in order in
either spelling now, so the near-miss rule holds for every word, and a
misspelled word with an accent or in another alphabet no longer lists
fff-search's far misses among the names that have such letters.

A word typed without accents found a name that has them only where
the name stores them apart, because its letters are then in the name
by themselves: `resume` listed a `Résumé.pdf` made in Finder and not
one that came from another system. Comparing in one spelling alone
would have taken the first away too. So a hit also has its path
without accents (`RawHit::bare`, made by `plain`), and `looked_in`
chooses between the two: a word typed without accents is looked for
there, and a word that has one in the path as it is spelled. That goes
for the check, for near misses, where an accent no longer counts as a
typo against a word typed without any (`bjrok` finds `Björk`), for
apps, for a one-letter search and for `changes_for`. Ranking bonuses
and quoted words go by the letters as typed, so the names that have
the word as typed stay ahead. Where a name stores an accent in one
character with its letter, fff-search hands the name over for such a
word only as one of its typo matches, which it does while no more than
two of the word's letters are missing, or more in a long first word:
`elephant` finds `éléphant.txt` either way, and `repete` finds
`répété.txt` only where its accents are stored apart.

The highlight is still positions in the name as it is stored.
`letters` gives each character of a name as it is compared, with the
place in the name of the letter it belongs to. An accent stored apart
belongs to the letter before it, and the parts of a Korean syllable
make one letter. `highlight` is the same greedy match over those, so
what is lit is whole letters, and a letter typed without its accent is
lit with it. For 400,000 random names and words without accents it
returned what the old one did.

`rerank` reads a hit's name from the end of `rel` (`RawHit::name`),
which is compared already, where it lowercased the name once more.

Cost, in the debug build, each search timed as the least of ten runs
(six in the last tree). The cargo registry has 49,518 files, all but
nine named in ASCII. 400 searches of it (50 queries as Any Kind, files
only, Folder and Code, with Allow typos on and off) took 12.74 s
before and 12.87 s after. 383 list the same items in the same order
with the same scores and highlights; the other 17 differ as much
between two runs of the same code (more equal matches than a search
ranks). The same 400 searches of the demo folder list what they
listed. Text in ASCII is not respelled. In a tree of 28,581 files made
for this, four fifths of whose paths are not ASCII (accents on every
vowel, stored both ways, and Korean, Chinese and Japanese names), a
word without accents took 40 ms where it took 21, a one-letter search
299 for 106, and a word with accents, which is searched twice, 130 for
15. That overstates it: the dev profile optimizes fff-search and not
icu_normalizer, which takes 6.8 µs here to respell a path that is
lowercased in 0.2. No release build was made.

Not done: `fff files` and `fff dirs` still ask fff-search in the
spelling typed alone, though the app names `fff dirs` adds are
compared as above. A search with two accented words finds a path that
stores one of them each way only through fff-search's typo matching.
A glob with such a letter still finds nothing in names that store it
as one character while letter case is ignored: `fold_case` makes the
letter a set, and fff-search's globs match a set byte by byte.

Changelog (Fixed): A name with accented letters is found however it
stores them. A name can keep a letter such as `é` as one character or
as the letter followed by its accent, which is how macOS stores the
names made in Finder and in most apps, and a search typed one way
didn't match a name stored the other. `señor` could miss `señor.txt`,
or list it only among loose typo matches without a highlight, and
never with **Allow typos** off, in a quoted word, or for an app. Both
spellings now match, with the word highlighted, and Korean names
stored as the parts of their syllables are found the same way. A word
typed without accents finds the names that have them, after the names
that have the word as typed: `resume` finds `Résumé.pdf`, which it did
only for names stored the macOS way. In a name that stores each accent
in one character with its letter, that works for up to two accented
letters in a word. A word typed with an accent finds only the names
that have it, and quoted words are compared letter for letter. A
misspelled word with an accent or in another alphabet now lists only
near misses, as other words do, and what a search opened is remembered
for such names too. `fff find` matches the same way.

